<p>The UK imported roughly USD 16.9 million of Indian fruit under HS 0804.50 in calendar 2024. That puts it in the premium-market tier alongside the US and Germany. Established UK Alphonso demand is driven largely by South Asian diaspora communities and, increasingly, by wider specialty-food retail. The HS heading also includes guava and mangosteen, so the figure is not mango-only. Mango is understood to make up the large majority of the value, but the classification does not allow a more precise claim. This guide is for UK importers, wholesalers and specialty retailers planning an April-July programme. It covers variety and grade specification, the documents each side handles, how price behaves, and the air-versus-sea decision.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>The WITS/UN Comtrade dataset for calendar 2024 puts total Indian exports under HS 0804.50 at USD 154.4 million worldwide, about 107,295 MT. Read the table above with one pattern in mind. Unit value varies sharply by destination: the United States imports at roughly USD 2.88 per kg and Saudi Arabia at about USD 0.90 per kg. This is not the same mango priced differently. It is two structurally different trades sharing one customs code. Western markets, the UK included, receive premium-variety fruit, mostly air-freighted and above all Alphonso and Kesar, sold into specialty and high-end retail. Gulf markets receive a higher-volume mix, often sea-freighted, at lower prices.</p>

<h2>Variety and grade specification for a UK order</h2>
<p>A mango quote is only comparable to another if both state the same variety, count grade and ripeness stage. Specify these items:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Variety</strong>: Alphonso (Hapus) or Kesar. Each has a distinct flavour and market position, and Alphonso commands the premium in most UK specialty channels.</li>
  <li><strong>Size/count grading</strong>: 8-10, 10-12 or 12-14 count per 3-4kg box, matched to your retail or food-service format. Larger, more uniform fruit sits at the top of the price range.</li>
  <li><strong>Eating quality</strong>: Brix 18%+ at ripeness for Alphonso. The fruit should be free from fruit-fly damage, black spot, bruising and split skin.</li>
  <li><strong>Ripeness at dispatch</strong>: set by the transit method, colour-turning for air and mature-green for sea. The comparison table at the end of this guide sets out the air-versus-sea trade-off.</li>
  <li><strong>Packing</strong>: 3kg, 4kg or 5kg single-layer cushioned corrugated cartons, or telescopic export cartons for premium air-freight orders. Single-layer packing matters because stacked fruit bruises.</li>
  <li><strong>Pesticide residue testing</strong>: strongly recommended before shipment because UK maximum residue limits are strict.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Season: a hard constraint, not a soft one</h2>
<p>Alphonso and Kesar mango are available from April to July. There is no meaningful off-season supply of fresh fruit, so this is not a product where enough notice lets us source it at any time. Early- and late-season fruit behave differently from mid-season peak volume in both price and consistency. If you are planning a UK mango programme, build your buying calendar around this window and book delivery weeks early. Our <a href="/resources/harvest-calendar/">harvest calendar</a> shows the wider seasonal picture across our product range.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>For the UK, your importer or their agent files the IPAFFS pre-notification before the consignment arrives. The details on the phytosanitary certificate have to match the consignment and the pre-notification exactly, because a mismatch holds fruit at the border, and for a perishable product that costs shelf life. Where the destination rules require hot water treatment, the HWT record goes with the phytosanitary paperwork. A pre-shipment pesticide residue report is not always mandatory, but it is the practical safeguard against a residue finding after landing. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote a flat per-kilogram number for mango, because the two logistics options alone put quotes on completely different footing before grade or variety even enter the picture. Air freight vs. sea freight is the single biggest price driver — air-freighted premium Alphonso carries the cost of same-day transit and lands at retail-grade freshness, while sea-freighted fruit in a reefer container costs less per kilogram but demands more ripeness-management discipline and a longer sell-through window. Beyond freight method, four things move the number: variety (Alphonso commands a premium over Kesar in most Western markets), size/count grade (larger, more uniform fruit costs more), the Konkan coast and Gujarat harvest timing and weather within the April-July season (early- and late-season fruit price differently than mid-season peak volume), and prevailing INR/GBP exchange rates plus current air or reefer freight rates.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific variety, size grade, freight method and delivery week — never as a flat seasonal-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: variety preference, size/count grade, air or sea freight, quantity, and your target delivery week within the season.</p>
