<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>Fruit-fly interception has real history on this exact product and route, and it's worth being direct about it rather than pretending the risk doesn't exist. The EU temporarily suspended imports of Indian mango (along with several other vegetables) in 2014 after repeated fruit-fly interceptions at the border, and the suspension was lifted later that year once India strengthened pre-export treatment — hot water treatment (HWT) among the key measures adopted. Current UK/EU risk is well managed through HWT where required and rigorous pre-shipment inspection, but the history is exactly why phytosanitary compliance on this specific product isn't treated as a formality. Ask any supplier directly whether HWT is applied and whether their pre-shipment inspection actually catches defects before loading, not just on paper.</p>
<p>The second recurring issue is ripeness mismatch at arrival — mango is climacteric, meaning it continues to ripen after harvest, and dispatching fruit at the wrong ripeness stage for your specific transit time is one of the most common causes of buyer disappointment. Air freight (about a day in transit) can ship fruit closer to ripe; sea freight (18-24 days) needs mature-green fruit dispatched deliberately under-ripe so it reaches usable ripeness on arrival rather than over-ripening in the container. We calibrate ripeness at dispatch to your actual transit method and time — a supplier quoting one ripeness standard regardless of freight method is a warning sign, not a convenience.</p>
<p>Third, size/count consistency within a carton matters more for this product than buyers often expect — mixed sizing inside a single box is a common quality complaint on under-specified orders, since retail and food-service buyers typically want visual and portion consistency across a batch.</p>
<p>What first-time UK buyers most often get wrong: underestimating how short the shelf life and selling window actually are once the fruit lands, especially for air-freighted stock that arrives closer to ripe. Plan your sell-through and any onward distribution before the container clears customs, not after — a few days' delay in distribution can meaningfully shorten your usable retail window on a product with only a 2-3 week reefer shelf life at best.</p>
